const faq = [
  { q: 'How do ravens stay warm in winter?', a: 'Ravens maintain body temperature through several mechanisms: thick insulating plumage (the multiple feather layers trap warm air against the body); reduced activity during the coldest hours (roosting conserves energy); communal roosting (body heat from multiple birds in a sheltered site reduces individual heat loss); and increased metabolic rate in cold conditions (burning more calories to generate heat). Ravens also have feathered legs and bill-bristles that reduce heat loss from exposed extremities — adaptations that differ from tropical corvids that have bare leg scales.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens eat in winter?', a: 'Ravens are opportunistic omnivores that shift their diet in winter toward whatever is available: primarily carrion (winter-killed ungulates, frozen fish, road-killed animals) supplemented by cached food items, refuse and food waste from human settlements, frozen berries and plant material, and occasional small prey. The winter diet is richer in fat than the summer diet — ravens seek out lipid-rich food (bone marrow, fat deposits, organ tissue) which provides more energy per gram than protein. The wolf-raven relationship is particularly important in winter, when wolf kills provide access to large carcasses that ravens cannot open alone.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens migrate south for winter?', a: 'Most Common Ravens do not migrate south for winter. They are year-round residents throughout their range, including in boreal forests, alpine zones, and arctic regions where winter conditions are severe. Some far-northern individuals (Greenland, high arctic Canada) may drift southward during the harshest periods, but this is not regular seasonal migration with predictable routes. The raven\'s dietary flexibility — particularly its ability to exploit carrion and human food waste year-round — makes migration unnecessary for a species that can find food in almost any winter environment.' },
];
